 within a full backup chain to expire before the entire chain can be removed. This means we are unable to free up storage through Nakivo at this time.\n\n \n\nHowever, some objects in the bucket should already have their immutability period expired. Could you please help investigate whether these expired objects are still occupying storage and why they have not been reclaimed? We believe this may be the root cause of the storage discrepancy we reported earlier.\n\n \n\nPlease let us know if you need any additional information from us.\n\n \n\nThank you.\n\nExpand Post\n1 view\n\ninfo@tsw\nWrite a comment...\nWrite a comment...\n\nRahul Grewal (Employee) sent an email.\n\n46m ago\nEmail\nTo: info@tsw\nView Details\n\nExpand Email\nImage file\nScreenshot 2026-06-22 144031.png\n\ninfo@tsw\nWrite a comment...\nWrite a comment...\n\ninfo@tsw (Customer)\n\n2h ago\nHi Rahul,\n\n \n\nPlease find the configuration details below:\n\n \n\n1. Bucket Versioning\n\nVersioning is currently Enabled on the \"tswbackup\" bucket.\n\n \n\n2. Object Lock\n\nObject Lock is Enabled. However, Default Object Retention is disabled, so no default retention period has been set at the bucket level.\n\n \n\n3. Lifecycle Policy (\"delete-old-versions\")\n\nThe policy is configured to apply to all objects in the bucket with the following actions:\n\nPermanently delete noncurrent versions of objects — 1 day after becoming noncurrent\nDelete expired object delete markers — Enabled\nDelete incomplete multipart uploads — Not enabled\nScreenshots are attached for reference.\n\nGiven this configuration, could you advise whether retention settings at the individual object level may be preventing permanent deletion, and whether this could be the reason storage has not been fully reclaimed?\n\n \n\nThank you.\n\nExpand Post\n1 comment\n2 views\n\ninfo@tsw (Customer)\n\n2 hours ago\nPlease check attachments.\n\n\ninfo@tsw\nWrite a comment...\nWrite a comment...\n\nRahul Grewal (Employee) sent an email.\n\n3